WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:04.640
So far, with functions, we have seen&nbsp;
how to call a function and use arguments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.680
within a function. In Python, you can set&nbsp;
default values for function arguments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.080
which will be used if the caller does&nbsp;
not provide a value for them. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:22.400
an argument may use a default value, such as&nbsp;
when a sales tax rate is one predominant rate or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:30.160
in the case of gaming and this 422-default file,&nbsp;
the usual multiplier on a score is 1.5. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.360
to save time and potential errors in calling&nbsp;
functions, we could make 1.5 the default value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:42.960
for the multiplier argument. To make that happen,&nbsp;
I will go to the function definition on line 1 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:53.200
type equal sign 1.5 after the multiplier&nbsp;
argument. Then, to test this, on line 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:01:01.400
I will remove the second argument from the&nbsp;
function call. I will run the code, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:10.120
first print score is perfect as it took the 3000&nbsp;
and multiplied it by 1.5. Though not common for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:16.560
smaller functions, one could specify the order&nbsp;
of the arguments used when calling a function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.080
The use of default and keyword arguments often&nbsp;
depends on the function's complexity and purpose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:27.080
not necessarily its size. To illustrate this&nbsp;
concept, I will add another print statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:35.760
on line 6 that calls the total score function,&nbsp;
specifying the name and value of each argument as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:43.360
keyword arguments. A developer may use this method&nbsp;
of calling a function to either be more specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.400
about displaying which arguments are being used&nbsp;
or perhaps because the data needed for a function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:57.880
are listed on a form in this order. Whatever&nbsp;
the case may be, I will rerun this code, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:05.640
the function returns three total scores, all with&nbsp;
the function called in slightly different ways.

